{"type":"message","id":"d4991961","parentId":"9a3256a7","timestamp":"2026-05-03T08:18:05.216Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"AleX wants me to create another version of the app, still for this first iteration, but built with DeepSeek V4 Code. He also wants it to have agent capabilities and an MCP protocol for bidirectional communication with other services.\n\nLet me think about this:\n\n1. **Built with DeepSeek V4 Code** - This means using the model as a coding agent, not just a subagent. Since the subagent approach failed before (provider rejected the tool schema), I need to find another way to use DeepSeek V4 Code.\n\n2. **Agent capabilities + MCP protocol** - This is a significant architectural addition:\n   - MCP (Model Context Protocol) server that allows the Ensoria OS to communicate with other services\n   - Agent capabilities - the ability for the system to act as an AI agent, process tasks, etc.\n\nWait, I should think about this differently. The user is asking me to build a version using DeepSeek V4 Code as the coding assistant. Since spawning subagents with DeepSeek V4 Code failed, let me think about what approach could work.\n\nActually, looking back at the error: \"provider rejected the request schema or tool payload\" - this happened when trying to spawn DeepSeek V4 Code as a subagent with tool calling capabilities. But what if I try a different approach?\n\nLet me try using the ACP route - spawn an ACP session with DeepSeek V4 Code as the agent. But wait, DeepSeek isn't an ACP agent type. It's a model provider.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider. The user said \"costruisci con deep seek v4 code\" - they want me to use DeepSeek V4 Code as the model to BUILD the code. Since I can't spawn it as a subagent, maybe I should:\n\n1. Switch my own model to DeepSeek V4 Code and write the code myself\n2. Or find another way to use DeepSeek V4 Code\n\nActually, looking at the model aliases:\n- DeepSeek V4 Code: deepseek/deepseek-v4-code\n\nThis is available as a model in my configuration. Let me try spawning a subagent with this model but with a much simpler task - maybe the previous failure was because the task was too large/complex for the subagent runtime.\n\nWait, actually the error was \"provider rejected the request schema or tool payload\" which might mean the model doesn't support subagent tool calls. Let me try a simpler approach:\n\nActually, I think the most practical approach is to:\n1. Write a comprehensive task specification\n2. Spawn a subagent with deepseek-v4-code but with a minimal structured task (no tool definitions in the task itself)\n\nLet me try a different pattern. Instead of asking the subagent to use tools (which involves file operations), let me give it a very clear task to write the code.\n\nActually, let me just try spawning again with a simpler approach and see if it works. If not, I'll write the MCP + agent version myself using DeepSeek V4 Code's context by switching my model.\n\nActually, I think the best approach here is:\n\n1. Switch my own session model to `deepseek/deepseek-v4-code`\n2. Write the new version myself with that model's coding strengths\n3. Build the MCP server + agent capabilities\n\nLet me set the session model to deepseek/deepseek-v4-code and build the enhanced version with MCP.\n\nWait, but the user said \"costruisci con deep seek v4 code\" - they want the code to be BUILT using that model. If I set my session model to DeepSeek V4 Code, then I AM building with that model. That makes sense.\n\nLet me try spawning a subagent one more time with a very simple task, and if it fails, I'll just switch model and write it myself.\n\nActually, let me think about what the \"new version\" with MCP + agent capabilities means architecturally:\n\n**MCP Server** - The app needs an MCP server that:\n- Exposes tools/resources via MCP protocol\n- Allows other AI agents to interact with the CRM data\n- Bidirectional communication (other services can query, and the system can push updates)\n\n**Agent Capabilities** - The system should:\n- Have an agent loop for processing tasks\n- Be able to use tools\n- Have some level of autonomous task processing\n\nFor the Ensoria OS CRM, MCP could mean:\n- An MCP server that exposes CRM operations (CRUD people/projects) as tools\n- Other agents can query \"what projects are in Contact stage?\" or \"create a new person\"\n- The system could have webhook/subscription capabilities for push notifications\n\nLet me try spawning with DeepSeek V4 Code.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_00_04gFdJv5CLiQbAWAltN05652","name":"session_status","arguments":{"model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-code"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"deepseek","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","usage":{"input":457,"output":1051,"cacheRead":129920,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":131428,"cost":{"input":0.00006398000000000001,"output":0.00029428,"cacheRead":0.00363776,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.003996019999999999}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1777796265410,"responseId":"e9037e99-9a2b-4d22-8814-af938b5070af"}}
